# Making `fcb_cpp` Self-Contained (No System OpenSSL Required)

## Background

The vcpkg curated registry rejects prebuilt ports that install `.a` libraries **and** depend on
other vcpkg ports (like `openssl`) at link time. The `fcb_cpp` static library currently requires
`libssl` on Linux, blocking acceptance.

**Good news:** Your Rust code already uses `reqwest` with `rustls-tls` (pure-Rust TLS — no
OpenSSL). The system OpenSSL requirement is almost certainly coming from a **transitive dependency**
being pulled into the `fcb_cpp` static build even though it isn't needed.

---

## Step 1 — Identify the actual culprit

Run this from `src/rust/` to see exactly which crate is pulling in `openssl-sys`:

```bash
cargo tree -p fcb_cpp --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu | grep -E "openssl|native-tls|hyper-tls"
```

Common suspects given your workspace:

| Crate | Why it might pull OpenSSL |
|---|---|
| `http-range-client` | May default to `native-tls` in some feature combos |
| `reqwest` | Safe — you use `rustls-tls` — but check nothing overrides it |
| `proj` | Binds to the PROJ C library, which links OpenSSL |
| `bson` / `serde_cbor` | Unlikely but possible via `ring` or `native-tls` |

---

## Step 2 — Fix: Audit `fcb_core`'s feature flags for `fcb_cpp`

`fcb_cpp/Cargo.toml` depends on `fcb_core` without specifying features:

```toml
# src/rust/fcb_cpp/Cargo.toml  (current)
[dependencies]
fcb_core = { workspace = true }
```

`fcb_core` has a `default = ["http"]` feature, which activates `http-range-client` with
`reqwest-async`. Even if you don't use HTTP in the C++ bindings, you're compiling it in.

**Fix — disable default features for `fcb_cpp`:**

```toml
# src/rust/fcb_cpp/Cargo.toml  (after)
[dependencies]
fcb_core = { workspace = true, default-features = false }
```

This removes the `http` feature (and therefore the entire `reqwest` + `http-range-client` chain)
from the static library. The C++ bindings only do local file I/O — they don't need HTTP.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> After this change, verify your C++ reader still compiles and the API surface you expose
> in `fcb.h` still works. If any C++ API calls an HTTP code path, you'll get a linker error
> pointing you to what needs adjusting.

---

## Step 3 — Fix: Make `http-range-client` explicitly use `rustls`

In the workspace `Cargo.toml`, `http-range-client` is declared without a TLS backend:

```toml
# src/rust/Cargo.toml  (current)
http-range-client = { version = "0.9.0", default-features = false }
```

When `fcb_core` re-enables it with `features = ["reqwest-async"]`, confirm that the transitive
`reqwest` pull uses **your** workspace `reqwest` (with `rustls-tls`). This should already be
the case because of Cargo's workspace dep resolution, but make it explicit:

```toml
# src/rust/Cargo.toml  (after — workspace.dependencies)
http-range-client = { version = "0.9.0", default-features = false, features = ["reqwest-async"] }
```

Then in `fcb_core/Cargo.toml` remove the `features = ["reqwest-async"]` override so it inherits
from the workspace definition cleanly.

---

## Step 4 — Add a `.cargo/config.toml` assertion (optional but recommended)

Add this to `src/rust/.cargo/config.toml` to make the CI build **fail loudly** if OpenSSL
is ever introduced again:

```toml
# src/rust/.cargo/config.toml
[build]
# Ensure the fcb_cpp crate never links against system OpenSSL.
# If this starts failing, run: cargo tree -p fcb_cpp | grep openssl
rustflags = []
```

And add a CI check (see Step 6).

---

## Step 5 — Update the GitHub Actions release workflow for `fcb_cpp`

In your release workflow (`.github/workflows/`), the Linux build step probably installs
`libssl-dev`. Once the dependency is removed, drop that step:

```yaml
# .github/workflows/release.yml  (remove or guard this)
- name: Install OpenSSL          # ← DELETE this step for the fcb_cpp job
  run: sudo apt-get install -y libssl-dev
```

If you have separate jobs for `fcb_cpp` and `fcb_cli`, only the **CLI job** may still need
OpenSSL (if the CLI uses `proj` or other C bindings). Keep it there, remove it from the
C++ bindings job.

---

## Step 6 — Add a CI smoke test to verify no OpenSSL linkage

Add a step to the `fcb_cpp` release job on Linux:

```yaml
- name: Verify no OpenSSL linkage in libfcb_cpp.a
  run: |
    # nm lists symbols; if openssl is linked in, you'll see SSL_ symbols
    if nm target/release/libfcb_cpp.a 2>/dev/null | grep -q "SSL_"; then
      echo "ERROR: libfcb_cpp.a links OpenSSL — check cargo tree -p fcb_cpp"
      exit 1
    fi
    echo "OK: no OpenSSL symbols found"
```

---

## Step 7 — Update `INSTALL.md`

If Steps 2–5 succeed, remove the OpenSSL prerequisite block from `src/cpp/INSTALL.md`:

```markdown
<!-- REMOVE this entire block -->
> **Linux prerequisite:** OpenSSL development libraries are required at link time. Install with:
> ```bash
> sudo apt-get install libssl-dev
> ```
```

And update the CMake example to remove the `find_package(OpenSSL)` / `OpenSSL::SSL` lines
from the Linux section.

---

## Expected outcome after these changes

| Platform | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Linux x86_64 | Needs `libssl-dev` at link time | ✅ Fully self-contained |
| Linux aarch64 | Needs `libssl-dev` at link time | ✅ Fully self-contained |
| macOS (any arch) | ✅ Already self-contained | ✅ No change |
| Windows x86_64 | ✅ Already self-contained | ✅ No change |

Once done, update the vcpkg port's `vcpkg.json` to remove the OpenSSL dependency:

```diff
  "dependencies": [
-   {
-     "name": "openssl",
-     "platform": "linux"
-   }
  ]
```

And update `unofficial-flatcitybuf-config.cmake.in` to remove the `find_dependency(OpenSSL)`
call and the Linux `INTERFACE_LINK_LIBRARIES` for `OpenSSL::SSL;OpenSSL::Crypto`.
